  "live_records": {
    "q-143477": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143477",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{80}$ be a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =150$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =40.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $140$ and $148.$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-2.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0099\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0096\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0094\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-2.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0129\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0125\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0122\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-2.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0166\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0162\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0158\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n\n<th>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3300\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3669\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3632\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3594\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4052\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4013\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3974\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=80,\\,\\mu =150,\\,\\sigma =40.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(150,\\frac{{40}^{2}}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(150,20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(140<\\overset{―}{X}<148).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(140<\\overset{―}{X}<148) & =P(\\frac{140-150}{\\sqrt{20}}<Z<\\frac{148-150}{\\sqrt{20}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\sqrt{5}<Z<-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.24<Z<-0.45) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.45)-P(Z<-2.24) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.45)-Φ(-2.24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.24)=0.0125, \\\\ Φ(-0.45)=0.3264.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(140<\\overset{―}{X}<148) & =Φ(-0.45)-Φ(-2.24) \\\\  & =0.3264-0.0125 \\\\  & =0.3139.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
