    "q-143520": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143520",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.3$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{96}$ be a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population with the probability mass function given above.  Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is greater than $3.$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. * \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=96,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 0.3+4\\cdot 0.25+6\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0+0.6+1+1.2 \\\\  & =2.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{6}^{2}\\cdot 0.2)-{2.8}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+1.2+4+7.2)-7.84 \\\\  & =4.56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.8,\\frac{4.56}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.8,0.0475).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>3) & ≈P(Z>\\frac{3-2.8}{\\sqrt{0.0475}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>0.92) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.92) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.92).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.92)=0.8212.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>3) & =1-Φ(0.92) \\\\  & =1-0.8212 \\\\  & =0.1788.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
