    "q-143524": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143524",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ be a random sample of size $n=100$ from a population with the probability mass function given above.  Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $7.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0606\\,$ | $\\,0.0594\\,$ | $\\,0.0582\\,$ | $\\,0.0571\\,$ | $\\,0.0559\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0735\\,$ | $\\,0.0721\\,$ | $\\,0.0708\\,$ | $\\,0.0694\\,$ | $\\,0.0681\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0885\\,$ | $\\,0.0869\\,$ | $\\,0.0853\\,$ | $\\,0.0838\\,$ | $\\,0.0823\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=100,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.2+5\\cdot 0.4+10\\cdot 0.2+20\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0+2+2+4 \\\\  & =8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{5}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{10}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{20}^{2}\\cdot 0.2)-{8}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+10+20+80)-64 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(8,\\frac{46}{100}) \\\\  & ∼N(8,0.46).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<7) & ≈P(Z<\\frac{7-8}{\\sqrt{0.46}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1.47) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.47).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.47)=0.0708.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<7) & =Φ(-1.47) \\\\  & =0.0708.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
